  "id": "signup_percent_change",
  "notes": "Reproduces in-head arithmetic: after two monthly aggregates come back from SQL the model must not compute the percent change itself. The synthetic execute_sql serves July 2026 = 12,480 and August 2026 = 13,977 signups for the common query shapes (month GROUP BY, one-row conditional counts, single-month counts, a LAG/percent computed in SQL). The exact change is 11.995192307692308% so a rounded figure is only accepted when it rounds from a tool value. Computing the percent in SQL is acceptable, so a missing calculate call is soft; an ungrounded figure is hard. Approximation markers (~, about, roughly) are judged by numeric_grounding rather than a blanket regex: an earlier CI run wrote '~28.6%' for a calculate result of 28.587 (result_display 28.6), which is rounding presentation of a grounded figure, not in-head arithmetic; only a hedged figure no tool supports fails (ungrounded_approximation, hard). an earlier CI run showed why the patterns must be anchored to month grain: a July 1-7 daily sanity-check query matched the generic GROUP BY pattern, was served the two monthly rows, and the model then refused to state the percent because the tool looked like placeholder data. Daily, weekly, sub-month, and single-day shapes now return a projection_not_modeled limitation instead. The one required_final_pattern (a percent figure) is promoted to hard via violation_severity because stating the percent change is the point of the scenario, not incidental coverage: an answer that gives both monthly totals but never states the percent has not answered the question.",
